WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.450
Hi there!

00:00:01.450 --> 00:00:07.200
We need to talk about GAN generated faces
after we spotted this paper beautifully entitled

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.639
“Eyes tell all.”

00:00:08.639 --> 00:00:09.639
Aaaw.

00:00:09.639 --> 00:00:11.839
When did paper titles become this catchy?

00:00:11.839 --> 00:00:17.010
I personally do not have any trouble with
making research sexy, as long as there is

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:19.600
no “All you need” in titles anymore.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.560
As you have already guessed, this paper is
highlighting that GAN generated images (at

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:25.930
least with StyleGAN2)

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:31.120
do not render the pupil accurately enough
and therefore, one can build a GAN-generated

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:34.180
face detector upon this feature.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:38.590
Ms. Coffee Bean saw that this paper received
an enormous amount of attention

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:44.060
on Twitter with reactions ranging from dismissing
the importance of this pupil feature,

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:49.460
because the next GAN generation will fix this
problem anyway, to cases of surprise that

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:52.900
GANs did not capture this human feature!

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:59.190
So, what are the implications of this paper
that highlights the pupil feature in particular?

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:04.549
And more generally, how to tell GAN-generated
images apart from real ones?

00:01:04.549 --> 00:01:07.420
This is what we will discuss in this AI Coffee
Break?

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:11.770
Hey, do you have your coffee ready, because
now we begin.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:15.549
This paper here highlights a problem in GAN-generated
faces:

00:01:15.549 --> 00:01:18.509
GAN stands for Generative Adversarial Networks.

00:01:18.509 --> 00:01:23.740
If you want a refresher of how GANs work to
produce images, check out our previous high-level

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:25.290
explanation about this.

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:32.240
So the authors investigate how GANs fail to
generate circular or elliptically-shaped pupils

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:33.600
for human eyes.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.579
Therefore, the authors propose a method to
automatically estimate the shape of pupils

00:01:38.579 --> 00:01:41.700
and determine if it is human-like enough.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.990
Since GANs tend to generate weird pupil shapes,
this should automatically detect whether the

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:50.859
depicted person is real or generated by a
GAN.

00:01:50.859 --> 00:01:54.069
But Ms. Coffee Bean sees here two categories
of problems.

00:01:54.069 --> 00:01:58.210
Let’s get to problem number one.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:05.509
The paper is framed like GANs in general have
this property of generating weirdly shaped

00:02:05.509 --> 00:02:06.509
pupils.

00:02:06.509 --> 00:02:11.860
But if we look closely into the experimental
section, we see that the authors have quantitatively

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:16.010
estimated this property only with StyleGAN2.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:21.959
So to generalize to “GAN-generated” in
general is a bit of a sneaky exaggeration.

00:02:21.959 --> 00:02:27.230
Do not get us wrong here: It is absolutely
the case that many other GANs have the same

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:29.680
problems with pupils too.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.270
At least when we zoom in a lot.

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:36.349
For Ms. Coffee Bean, the pupil problem is
not that strong.

00:02:36.349 --> 00:02:41.269
Which is strange, because the authors
reportedly used exactly the same cool website,

00:02:41.269 --> 00:02:47.230
thispersoendoesnotexist.com (do check it out)
to retrieve some StyleGAN2 generations.

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:48.230
In any case…

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:52.599
GAN generation quality is not there yet where
details are rendered well,

00:02:52.599 --> 00:02:56.239
just look at the eyebrows and eyelashes too.

00:02:56.239 --> 00:03:01.470
These little details are easy to overlook
if these GAN-generated images are used in

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:05.430
settings where pictures are not looked at
in full detail,

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:11.800
for example in bot accounts on social media
where the profile picture is so tiny anyway.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:17.250
So understandably, there is need for an automatic
bot checker as the authors propose.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:23.100
But the argument of using this pupil-detector
widely grows weaker because StyleGAN2 won’t

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:25.620
be NVIDIAs last generation of GANs ever

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:29.090
and other parties are training powerful GANs
too.

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:34.610
Especially since GAN-generated face detection
is an arms race, now that the defenders know

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:39.530
of this eye pupil feature, the attackers will
do their best to make the right generations

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:43.930
in the next version,
circumventing automatic GAN generated face

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:44.930
detectors.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:47.239
So what we can learn from this paper so far,
is that

00:03:47.239 --> 00:03:52.540
when zooming in and looking at the tiny details,
we see problems in GAN generations.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:58.769
It’s not really news that tiny coherent
structure is hard to enforce, look at the

00:03:58.769 --> 00:04:00.349
details of hair,

00:04:00.349 --> 00:04:05.690
everything about the eyes,
teeth or tongue or lips for example.

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:08.140
Ms. Coffee Bean, should we write a paper now?

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:09.189
Lips tell all?

00:04:09.189 --> 00:04:10.349
Just kidding.

00:04:10.349 --> 00:04:16.780
But coherent structure over larger distances
is even harder, especially for elements with

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:20.200
highest diversity in the images in the training
set.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:25.790
We argue that pupils would be the first ones
to be fixed in GAN generation, really, because

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:29.070
that feature occurs in most of the faces.

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:34.630
But structures spanning longer distances in
the image that are only exhibited by a subset

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:37.570
of the data, is far more problematic.

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:41.420
Just look at the background that is highly
diverse in the training data and should be

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:43.560
diverse in the generations too.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:46.950
It seldomly makes sense, unless it’s uniform.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:49.970
Like where is this person even situated?

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:54.380
Or look at the ears for example which are
not often featured both in images because

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:57.870
of the lateral angle or hair covering them
up.

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:03.560
This picture was generated by StyleGAN2 too
and look how the ear lobes are at different

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:04.560
heights.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.690
But sure, all these problems are not that
easy for automatic detection with hand-crafted

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:11.900
features like the pupils are.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:18.710
A funny side-note, the problem with the StyleGAN
generating bad pupils is weird, perhaps unexpected.

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:26.490
But it’s well known that neural nets tend
to pay attention to very weird tiny features,

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:31.760
like tiny details between animal species when
it comes to their fur structure,

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:37.030
while being sloppy on more obvious features
for humans, like learning that dogs usually

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:38.490
have 4 legs.

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:41.330
They surprise us negatively, as much as positively.

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:46.440
Personally, I was astonished when learning
that neural nets even pay attention to visual

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:52.120
chirality, being able to tell whether an image
has been mirrored or not by judging from things

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:57.160
that are statistically more often encountered
in one way than in the other, like buttons

00:05:57.160 --> 00:05:58.490
on men’s shirts.

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:00.000
For more, check out our video.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:06.510
Now, let’s get to the problem number two
of this method.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:12.110
The authors acknowledge that detecting how
elliptical a pupil is, could discriminate

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:16.460
against people who just happen to have not
circular pupils.

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:20.920
The authors come with no real suggestion here
to circumvent the problem and say that this

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:25.270
is an infrequent phenomenon and “abnormal”
pupils were

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:32.530
not found in the real images of the dataset
they used, consisting of professional photographs!

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:36.830
That this dataset doesn’t contain abnormal
pupils does not mean anything to Ms. Coffee

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:40.970
Bean, since this could be due to a kind of
selection bias.

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:46.950
Therefore, she would like to stress the warning
here, not to use only an eye pupil normality

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:48.940
checker (if at all).

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:53.050
Otherwise, imagine people being told: Sorry,
we don’t allow you to create your Tinder

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:58.680
account because our anti-GAN AI tells us that
you have robot-shaped pupils.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:06:59.680
Bye!

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:01.500
So what to conclude?

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:07.490
That humans, given enough image resolution
and knowing where to look for errors, can

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:11.380
STILL know if an image is GAN generated or
not.

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:17.620
But the GAN that is making detection humanly
impossible, is not really that far away from

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:21.350
now, so we need automatic methods that detect
those.

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:28.130
A method based on strangely shaped pupils
is useful for now, for StyleGAN2 and its likes,

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:31.900
but is short-lived – like so many things
in machine learning.

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:36.430
What Ms. Coffee Bean likes about this paper
is that it puts things into perspective for

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:37.780
non-ML experts.

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:41.250
Let us know what you think about the paper
in the comments.

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:44.830
Like and subscribe.

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:51.100
Okay bye!

